.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-admin-wallets-deactivate - Switch a wallet off, on any account (write mode, audited)


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 admin wallets deactivate  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Switch a wallet off. It stops being offered to buyers, so no new payment can name it,
and webhooks stop being dispatched for its payments. Existing payments settle whatever
the switch says: they carry the address themselves.

.PP
This is the definitive revocation, for a leaked or lost key: a wallet an operator
switched off can be switched back on only by an operator ('rail0 admin wallets
activate'). Its own session cannot, where it could after switching itself off.

.PP
The command reads what still names the address first ('rail0 admin wallets usage') and
asks. The account's only active wallet is refused (422 last_active_wallet): the account
could no longer sign in.

.PP
A write: it needs write mode ('rail0 admin write-mode on').


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for deactivate

.PP
\fB-y\fP, \fB--yes\fP[=false]
	Skip the confirmation prompt (required to run non-interactively)


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--account\fP=""
	The account the wallets belong to (required)

.PP
\fB--agent\fP[=false]
	Agent mode: one JSON envelope on stdout, JSON events on stderr, no prompts (env: RAIL0_AGENT=1)

.PP
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 admin wallets deactivate 0x2D2B… --account 019e748b-…
  rail0 admin wallets deactivate 019f02aa-… --account 019e748b-… --yes
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-admin-wallets(1)\fP
